{
	title: 'Replace tool chevrons with message timestamps',
	spec: "Invariant: tool calls show their recorded start time in the same HH:MM format as other messages, through shared titles.time, in terminal and web including mobile; it is stable from first stream through saved history. Remove the decorative leading chevron. Web folded cards use their timestamp as the native named expansion button with aria-expanded, no empty icon-sized box; preserve 44px touch targets, header links as siblings, native keyboard activation, selection and retained card identity. The button inherits the transcript font, its text is left aligned, and headline separation is one monospace character like ordinary headers. Thinking and summary-folded prompts also replace their chevron with their recorded timestamp without repeating it in the visible headline. Every block and card, in terminal and web, starts with its time as an HH:MM prefix (with day and month first when not from today, as titles.time gives): prompts, messages from other sessions (waiting or delivered), queued rows, model text, thinking, tool calls, questions, commands, output, turn ends and dividers. Every shown item carries the ts of its record or event; a row the web sends optimistically carries its send time. All of them render the time through the same code (titles.time via titles.title; on web CardHeader), never a per-kind variant. No 'Details' or other stand-in label replaces the time. Command output uses the same shared title formatter and header/blank-row/body layout as other messages on web and terminal, rather than prepending its timestamp to Markdown body text. Preserve descriptions, commands, output, references and collapse behavior. Verify timestamp parity, header/body spacing and keyboard/link behavior in a real browser.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Tool calls already carry per-block start timestamps in history and live turns, but transcript.blockItems discarded them while retaining time for text/thinking. Preserve that same field for tools; no history migration or provider change. Command output separately concatenated its timestamp into Markdown while ordinary cards used titles.title and the common header. Route output through the existing common title and headed layouts; no extra timestamp formatter or renderer. Folded timestamp buttons inherit the transcript font, are left aligned and use a one-character header gap; preserve touch size and named native activation. Enter CDP activation requires text carriage return as well as its virtual key code.',
		'Browser checks at 390px touch and 1200px desktop verify identical tool, prompt and terminal HH:MM, shared output header inset, output body without duplicate time, inherited button font, 44px touch geometry, URL navigation without expansion and native Enter collapse. Terminal regression covers both Bash and other tool timestamps and ordinary/output header blank-row parity. Existing live-vs-snapshot transcript tests cover retained tool timestamps. Full ./test: 1337 passed, one skipped, four timing failures in max_tokens/refusal, two host transcript waits and browser streamed frame sample count; types and lint passed. Rerunning affected files plus changed areas: 108 passed, one browser frame sample count failure; host timing tests passed. The timestamp/link/keyboard browser regression passed in all final runs.',
		'Final spacing puts the one-character separator inside the timestamp button padding, rather than after it: its touch target includes useful text plus the separator, not an extra icon slot. Latest full ./test: 1339 passed, one skipped, two unrelated host timeouts (cross-tab login resume and completed transcript); all browser checks, TypeScript and lint passed.',
		"Every shown item carries ts: records always had it; transcript now copies it onto images, results, turn ends and dividers (compact, rebase), turn-end and tool-results events carry it, waiting inbox items keep theirs, and the web's optimistic Sending row carries its send time (never sent to the host). One-row blocks (turn end, divider, a result apart from its call) and the queued note lead with titles.stamp; headers use titles.title; prompt-change rows and notification entries use titles.time instead of their own formatters (notifications showed locale 12-hour time). Images stay inside their prompt's card with its time. CardHeader no longer falls back to 'Details'. replay.clock (the model's prompt stamps) stays separate: it is model input, not display.",
